{"data":{"diff_size":2327,"new_content":"Extension: \"Climb\" -- a game mode with levels\n\nOverview\nAnimator gains a game mode, chosen from a menu next to the normal free-drawing\nmode, with three levels (designed later). In each level, the player writes a draw\nfunction whose lines must climb from the bottom of the canvas to the top while\ndodging \"evil\" drawings: red paths, built into the level, that are drawn from the\ntop of the canvas toward the bottom as the animation plays. The normal animator\nkeeps working exactly as before when free-drawing mode is selected.\n\nThe anchor\nEach level starts the player at a point near the bottom of the canvas called the\nanchor. In game mode, draw code can read a built-in value `anchor`, a Point (like\nshape_slider is an Int), so a player can write, for example,\nPath(Line(anchor, Point(0, 300)), 0, 10, 3). The draw function's signature does not\nchange.\n\nMoving the anchor (in code)\nA player path only counts if it is connected: it must start at the anchor, or at a\npoint on one of the player's own connected paths that has already been drawn by the\ntime the new path starts. Starting a path from such a point is how the player moves\nthe anchor -- at any time, but only to a place where they already have a visible\nline. Paths that start anywhere else are drawn faded and do not count.\n\nWinning and losing\nThe player wins when a connected path reaches the top edge of the canvas. The player\nis caught when one of their connected paths touches a red path at a moment when both\nof the touching parts have been drawn (line thickness counts). Because both kinds of\ndrawing appear over time, the outcome is a race: whichever happens first -- reaching\nthe top or being caught -- ends the attempt. Timing a path's start and end is\ntherefore part of the strategy, not only its shape.\n\nWhen the attempt ends, the animation stops and a banner says \"Level complete!\" or\n\"Caught!\". Reset (or editing the code) retries the level. The anchor is shown on\nthe canvas as a small marker, player paths use the normal color, and evil paths are\ndrawn in red.\n\nImplementation notes\nThe levels (anchor start point and red paths) are Gleam data in a new module. The\nwin/hit times are computed from the paths' geometry and start/end times, and the app\nshows the result when the canvas clock reaches that time. `anchor` is bound as an\nextra built-in in the global frame, so the interpreter itself is unchanged.\n","new_content_size":2385,"new_hash":"5ad6b69ad1ed5bdea408f5b554b76327a7005bbe6502dec5c63d7993409700e6","old_hash":"46d8aa87a36b5275259b43a6ce7d0b397b7d83db5c4896269c63452a78fa68ac","operation":"modify","path":"extension.txt"},"hash":"484a5cffb9a9d2233b8c683251aefde143145d91203411562378c32373d627d4","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"08861791cd00b05e36296f5cd8fb90bbf118497812d92b7326a91536c2ab865c","seq":5064,"t":74508190,"wall":"2026-11-15T22:38:05.904Z"}
