{"data":{"diff_size":770,"new_content":"//// The rules of game mode (\"Climb\", described in extension.txt).\n////\n//// The player's code sees a built-in `anchor` Point. Its draw function's lines\n//// count only if they start at the anchor. An optional\n//// `fn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point` names the anchor's next position; the\n//// anchor moves there once a counting line has been drawn through it. The player\n//// wins when a line reaches the green goal bar along the top, and loses when one\n//// of their lines touches a red path while both are drawn, or touches the left or\n//// right edge of the canvas.\n////\n//// The player's paths are timed on the main clock (which the playback controls\n//// move), the red paths on the red clock (which runs on its own), and lines left\n//// behind by an anchor move are frozen, always fully drawn, until cleared.\n\nimport builtins\nimport drawing\nimport errors.{MissingFunction}\nimport evaluator.{VData, VInt}\nimport gleam/float\nimport gleam/int\nimport gleam/list\nimport gleam/option.{type Option, None, Some}\nimport mutable_dict\nimport parser\nimport shape.{type Path, type Point, Line, Path, Point}\n\n/// The y coordinate of the canvas's top edge.\npub const top = -500.0\n\n/// The y coordinate of the goal bar's lower edge: the bar fills the canvas from\n/// here up to the top, and a line that reaches it wins.\npub const goal = -460.0\n\n/// The x coordinate of the canvas's right edge (the left edge is at -edge). A\n/// line that touches either side edge loses.\npub const edge = 500.0\n\n/// What a frame of play can lead to.\npub type Event {\n  // A counting line reached the point move_anchor named.\n  AnchorReached(Point)\n  // A counting line reached the goal bar.\n  Won\n  // One of the player's lines touched a red path.\n  Caught\n  // One of the player's lines touched the left or right edge.\n  HitEdge\n}\n\n/// Everything check needs to judge a frame.\npub type Snapshot {\n  Snapshot(\n    anchor: Point,\n    // Where move_anchor says the anchor goes next, if anywhere.\n    target: Option(Point),\n    // The player's counting lines (those that start at the anchor), timed on\n    // the main clock.\n    lines: List(Path),\n    // The player's frozen old lines.\n    ghosts: List(Path),\n    // The level's red paths, timed on the red clock.\n    red: List(Path),\n  )\n}\n\n// ── Running the player's code ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/// Parse source with `anchor` bound, call draw on shape_slider, and call\n/// move_anchor on the anchor if the program defines it. Returns the paths and the\n/// anchor's next position, or a human-readable error message.\npub fn run(\n  source: String,\n  shape_slider: Int,\n  anchor: Point,\n) -> Result(#(List(Path), Option(Point)), String) {\n  let frame = builtins.global_frame()\n  let anchor_value = point_value(anchor)\n  mutable_dict.insert(frame, \"anchor\", anchor_value)\n  case parser.parse_program(source, frame) {\n    Error(msg) -> Error(msg)\n    Ok(program) ->\n      case drawing.try_draw(shape_slider, program) {\n        Error(err) -> Error(errors.error_message(err))\n        Ok(paths) ->\n          case evaluator.run(\"move_anchor\", [anchor_value], program) {\n            Ok(VData(\"Point\", [VInt(x), VInt(y)])) ->\n              Ok(#(paths, Some(Point(int.to_float(x), int.to_float(y)))))\n            Ok(_) -> Error(\"move_anchor must return a Point\")\n            // No move_anchor (with one argument): the anchor stays put.\n            Error(MissingFunction(\"move_anchor\", _)) -> Ok(#(paths, None))\n            Error(err) -> Error(errors.error_message(err))\n          }\n      }\n  }\n}\n\nfn point_value(p: Point) -> evaluator.Value {\n  VData(\"Point\", [VInt(float.round(p.x)), VInt(float.round(p.y))])\n}\n\n/// Whether a player's path counts: it must start at the anchor.\npub fn counts(path: Path, anchor: Point) -> Bool {\n  case path.shape {\n    Line(start, _) -> start == anchor\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Judging a frame ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/// What, if anything, has happened by main-clock time t and red-clock time\n/// red_t. Losing (being caught, then hitting an edge) outranks winning, which\n/// outranks moving the anchor. Line thickness counts throughout.\npub fn check(s: Snapshot, t: Float, red_t: Float) -> Option(Event) {\n  let current = list.filter_map(s.lines, visible(_, t))\n  let ghosts = list.filter_map(s.ghosts, visible(_, 0.0))\n  let mine = list.append(current, ghosts)\n  let reds = list.filter_map(s.red, visible(_, red_t))\n  let caught =\n    list.any(mine, fn(m) {\n      list.any(reds, fn(r) {\n        let reach = { m.thickness +. r.thickness } /. 2.0\n        segment_distance2(m.a, m.b, r.a, r.b) <=. reach *. reach\n      })\n    })\n  let hit_edge =\n    list.any(mine, fn(m) {\n      let half = m.thickness /. 2.0\n      float.min(m.a.x, m.b.x) -. half <=. 0.0 -. edge\n      || float.max(m.a.x, m.b.x) +. half >=. edge\n    })\n  let won =\n    list.any(current, fn(m) {\n      float.min(m.a.y, m.b.y) -. m.thickness /. 2.0 <=. goal\n    })\n  let moved = case s.target {\n    Some(target) ->\n      case distance2(target, s.anchor) >. 1.0 {\n        False -> None\n        True ->\n          case\n            list.any(current, fn(m) {\n              let reach = m.thickness /. 2.0 +. 1.0\n              point_segment_distance2(target, m.a, m.b) <=. reach *. reach\n            })\n          {\n            True -> Some(AnchorReached(target))\n            False -> None\n          }\n      }\n    None -> None\n  }\n  case caught, hit_edge, won {\n    True, _, _ -> Some(Caught)\n    _, True, _ -> Some(HitEdge)\n    _, _, True -> Some(Won)\n    _, _, False -> moved\n  }\n}\n\n/// The player's lines as drawn at time t, frozen: each becomes an instant path\n/// covering only the part drawn so far, and lines not yet started are dropped.\npub fn freeze(paths: List(Path), t: Float) -> List(Path) {\n  list.filter_map(paths, fn(p) {\n    case visible(p, t) {\n      Ok(seg) -> Ok(Path(Line(seg.a, seg.b), 0.0, 0.0, p.thickness))\n      Error(Nil) -> Error(Nil)\n    }\n  })\n}\n\n// ── Geometry ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// The drawn part of a line: from a to b, with its thickness.\ntype Segment {\n  Segment(a: Point, b: Point, thickness: Float)\n}\n\n// The part of path drawn by time t, matching what canvas.mjs shows: nothing\n// before it starts, then a growing piece from its start point. A path that has\n// no length yet (just started, rewound to its start, or from a point to itself)\n// counts as nothing drawn, so a lone anchor can't be caught, win, or move.\nfn visible(path: Path, t: Float) -> Result(Segment, Nil) {\n  case t <. path.start, path.shape {\n    True, _ -> Error(Nil)\n    False, Line(a, b) -> {\n      let duration = path.end -. path.start\n      let u = case duration <=. 0.0 {\n        True -> 1.0\n        False -> float.clamp({ t -. path.start } /. duration, 0.0, 1.0)\n      }\n      let tip = Point(a.x +. { b.x -. a.x } *. u, a.y +. { b.y -. a.y } *. u)\n      case tip == a {\n        True -> Error(Nil)\n        False -> Ok(Segment(a, tip, path.thickness))\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfn distance2(p: Point, q: Point) -> Float {\n  let dx = p.x -. q.x\n  let dy = p.y -. q.y\n  dx *. dx +. dy *. dy\n}\n\n// The squared distance from p to the segment from a to b.\nfn point_segment_distance2(p: Point, a: Point, b: Point) -> Float {\n  let dx = b.x -. a.x\n  let dy = b.y -. a.y\n  let length2 = dx *. dx +. dy *. dy\n  let u = case length2 == 0.0 {\n    True -> 0.0\n    False ->\n      float.clamp(\n        { { p.x -. a.x } *. dx +. { p.y -. a.y } *. dy } /. length2,\n        0.0,\n        1.0,\n      )\n  }\n  distance2(p, Point(a.x +. dx *. u, a.y +. dy *. u))\n}\n\n// The squared distance between segments p1-p2 and q1-q2: zero if they cross,\n// otherwise the smallest distance from an endpoint of one to the other.\nfn segment_distance2(p1: Point, p2: Point, q1: Point, q2: Point) -> Float {\n  let d1 = cross(q1, q2, p1)\n  let d2 = cross(q1, q2, p2)\n  let d3 = cross(p1, p2, q1)\n  let d4 = cross(p1, p2, q2)\n  case d1 *. d2 <. 0.0 && d3 *. d4 <. 0.0 {\n    True -> 0.0\n    False ->\n      list.fold(\n        [\n          point_segment_distance2(q1, p1, p2),\n          point_segment_distance2(q2, p1, p2),\n          point_segment_distance2(p2, q1, q2),\n        ],\n        point_segment_distance2(p1, q1, q2),\n        float.min,\n      )\n  }\n}\n\n// Which side of the line through o and a the point b is on (the z of the cross\n// product): positive one way, negative the other, zero on the line.\nfn cross(o: Point, a: Point, b: Point) -> Float {\n  { a.x -. o.x } *. { b.y -. o.y } -. { a.y -. o.y } *. { b.x -. o.x }\n}\n","new_content_size":8863,"new_hash":"1782e71bb0eb448ef4b005df58f93639111995ece0f355d69a99448295f9e63d","old_hash":"6e68d07ba409eb4a7539271a39af119035a21a5b146222ecf857856af0f6ef8f","operation":"modify","path":"src/game.gleam"},"hash":"843dc727c0efaa697abac25fc07da934e0ec46b3417d6a7972181d667d4c2bcd","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"f1e4b1800022f777a9a0060f1573007ba10d17c16082b99e78e2524ae3b2c30a","seq":5246,"t":77596498,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:34.033Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":152,"new_content":"//// Animator -- an application for program-generated drawings.\n\nimport builtins\nimport data\nimport drawing\nimport editor\nimport errors\nimport game\nimport gleam/bool\nimport gleam/float\nimport gleam/int\nimport gleam/list\nimport gleam/option.{type Option, None, Some}\nimport gleam/result\nimport gleam/string\nimport levels.{type Level}\nimport lustre\nimport lustre/attribute as attr\nimport lustre/effect.{type Effect}\nimport lustre/element.{type Element}\nimport lustre/element/html\nimport lustre/element/keyed\nimport lustre/event\nimport parser\nimport render\nimport shape.{type Path, type Point, Line, Path, Point}\nimport tutorial\n\n/// Your app's public address, once you've deployed it in Problem 13, such as\n/// \"https://your-username.github.io/animator/\".\npub const public_url = \"\"\n\n/// Set to True if you would like your app's URL included in the course gallery.\npub const include_app_in_gallery = False\n\n/// A link to your video from Problem 14, which anyone with the link can watch.\npub const video_url = \"\"\n\n/// Set to True if you would like your video included in the course gallery.\npub const include_video_in_gallery = False\n\n// Range and starting value of the shape slider.\nconst shape_slider_min = 0\n\nconst shape_slider_max = 100\n\nconst default_shape_slider = 10\n\n// Range of the speed slider, as a multiple of normal speed.\nconst speed_min = -6.0\n\nconst speed_max = 6.0\n\n// The menu value of the \"+ New drawing…\" option. Drawing names are never empty,\n// so it can't collide with one.\nconst new_drawing_option = \"\"\n\n// The code a new drawing starts with.\nconst new_drawing_code =\n  \"fn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\\n  [Path(Line(Point(0, 0), Point(100, 100)), 0, 10, 2)]\\n}\\n\"\n\n// Colors on the canvas.\nconst player_color = \"#cfe3ff\"\n\n// Game mode: player lines that don't start at the anchor (and so don't count),\n// old lines left behind by an anchor move, the red paths, the anchor marker and\n// the next anchor position, and the goal line along the top edge.\nconst faded_color = \"#4a5570\"\n\nconst ghost_color = \"#8a9cc0\"\n\nconst red_color = \"#ff4d4d\"\n\nconst anchor_color = \"#ffcb6b\"\n\nconst target_color = \"#8a7440\"\n\nconst goal_color = \"#4caf7a\"\n\n// ── Model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub type Model {\n  Model(\n    // The code in the editor.\n    source: String,\n    // The library: every drawing's #(name, source), in menu order.\n    drawings: List(#(String, String)),\n    // The name of the drawing open in the editor.\n    current: String,\n    // Bumped to rebuild the drawing menu, so it shows `current` again after\n    // the \"+ New drawing…\" option was picked (e.g. when the prompt is cancelled).\n    menu_revision: Int,\n    // Whether the code editor is shown.\n    code_open: Bool,\n    output: String,\n    // The value passed to draw as shape_slider.\n    shape_slider: Int,\n    // Animation speed: 1.0 is normal, 0.0 pauses, negative plays backward.\n    speed: Float,\n    // Whether the playback-controls box is expanded.\n    controls_open: Bool,\n    // ── Game mode (see game.gleam and extension.txt) ──\n    game_on: Bool,\n    // Every level's code, #(level name, source), in menu order.\n    level_codes: List(#(String, String)),\n    // The name of the level being played.\n    level: String,\n    // Where the player's lines must start.\n    anchor: Point,\n    // Where move_anchor says the anchor goes next, if anywhere.\n    target: Option(Point),\n    // The paths the player's draw returned for the current anchor.\n    stage: List(Path),\n    // The player's old lines, frozen when the anchor moved, until cleared.\n    ghosts: List(Path),\n    status: Status,\n    // The \"How to play\" guide's open step (counting from 0), if it is open.\n    guide: Option(Int),\n  )\n}\n\n/// How the current attempt at a level stands.\npub type Status {\n  Playing\n  LevelWon\n  // Lost, with the banner's title saying why.\n  LevelLost(reason: String)\n}\n\npub type Msg {\n  SetCode(String)\n\n  // Drawing library\n  SelectDrawing(String)\n  CreateDrawing(String)\n\n  // Sliders (the browser reports a range input's value as a string)\n  SetShapeSlider(String)\n  SetSpeed(String)\n\n  // Playback buttons\n  Reset\n  Play\n  Pause\n\n  // Layout controls\n  ToggleCode\n  ToggleControls\n\n  // Game mode\n  ToggleGame\n  SelectLevel(String)\n  RestartLevel\n  // Something happened in play, at this main-clock time.\n  GameEvent(game.Event, Float)\n\n  // The \"How to play\" guide\n  OpenGuide\n  CloseGuide\n  GuideNext\n  GuideBack\n  GuideToTutorial\n}\n\n// ── Init ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn init(_flags: Nil) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  // Reopen the saved library at the drawing that was open last (or the first).\n  let drawings = data.load_library()\n  let first = case drawings {\n    [#(name, _), ..] -> name\n    [] -> \"\"\n  }\n  let current = case data.load_current() {\n    Ok(name) ->\n      case list.key_find(drawings, name) {\n        Ok(_) -> name\n        Error(_) -> first\n      }\n    Error(_) -> first\n  }\n  let source = list.key_find(drawings, current) |> result.unwrap(\"\")\n  // Each level's saved code (or the starter code), and the level open last.\n  let level_codes =\n    list.map(levels.all(), fn(l) {\n      #(l.name, data.load_level_code(l.name) |> result.unwrap(l.starter))\n    })\n  let first_level = case level_codes {\n    [#(name, _), ..] -> name\n    [] -> \"\"\n  }\n  let level = case data.load_level() {\n    Ok(name) ->\n      case list.key_find(level_codes, name) {\n        Ok(_) -> name\n        Error(_) -> first_level\n      }\n    Error(_) -> first_level\n  }\n  let model =\n    Model(\n      source: source,\n      drawings: drawings,\n      current: current,\n      menu_revision: 0,\n      code_open: True,\n      output: \"\",\n      shape_slider: default_shape_slider,\n      speed: 1.0,\n      controls_open: True,\n      game_on: False,\n      level_codes: level_codes,\n      level: level,\n      anchor: Point(0.0, 0.0),\n      target: None,\n      stage: [],\n      ghosts: [],\n      status: Playing,\n      guide: None,\n    )\n  // Reopen game mode if it was on when the page was last used.\n  case data.load_game_on() {\n    True -> open_level(model, level)\n    False -> #(\n      model,\n      // Effects that happen automatically at the start go here.\n      effect.batch([\n        editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n        effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n      ]),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// Parse and evaluate a draw function at the given shape-slider value, returning\n// the rendered paths on success, or a human-readable message on failure.\nfn run_source(shape_slider: Int, source: String) -> Result(List(Path), String) {\n  case parser.parse_program(source, builtins.global_frame()) {\n    Error(msg) -> Error(msg)\n    Ok(program) ->\n      drawing.try_draw(shape_slider, program)\n      // Convert Error(EvalError) to Error(String) with a useful error message.\n      |> result.map_error(errors.error_message)\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Update ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn update(model: Model, msg: Msg) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case msg {\n    ToggleCode -> #(Model(..model, code_open: !model.code_open), effect.none())\n\n    ToggleControls -> #(\n      Model(..model, controls_open: !model.controls_open),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    // After each keystroke in the editor, save the code into the open drawing,\n    // then evaluate it and report errors.\n    // In game mode, the code is saved as the open level's instead.\n    SetCode(text) ->\n      case model.game_on {\n        False -> {\n          let drawings = replace_key(model.drawings, model.current, text)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            redraw(Model(..model, source: text, drawings: drawings))\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_library(drawings) }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n        True -> {\n          let level = model.level\n          let level_codes = replace_key(model.level_codes, level, text)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            redraw(Model(..model, source: text, level_codes: level_codes))\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_level_code(level, text) }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    // Picking \"+ New drawing…\" asks for a name; any other option opens that\n    // drawing.\n    SelectDrawing(value) ->\n      case value == new_drawing_option {\n        True -> #(\n          model,\n          effect.from(fn(dispatch) {\n            dispatch(CreateDrawing(data.prompt_drawing_name()))\n          }),\n        )\n        False -> open_drawing(model, value)\n      }\n\n    // A blank name (or a cancelled prompt) only puts the menu back; an existing\n    // name opens that drawing; a new name adds a drawing and opens it.\n    CreateDrawing(name) -> {\n      let name = string.trim(name)\n      case name, list.key_find(model.drawings, name) {\n        \"\", _ -> #(\n          Model(..model, menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1),\n          effect.none(),\n        )\n        _, Ok(_) -> open_drawing(model, name)\n        _, Error(_) -> {\n          let drawings =\n            list.append(model.drawings, [#(name, new_drawing_code)])\n          let #(model, open_effect) =\n            open_drawing(Model(..model, drawings: drawings), name)\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_library(drawings) }),\n              open_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Moving the shape slider re-runs draw on the new value.\n    SetShapeSlider(value) ->\n      case int.parse(value) {\n        Ok(n) -> redraw(Model(..model, shape_slider: n))\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n\n    SetSpeed(value) ->\n      case parse_number(value) {\n        Ok(rate) -> set_speed(model, rate)\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n\n    // Jump back to the start (empty canvas); playback continues at the\n    // current speed.\n    // In game mode this also clears the player's old lines.\n    Reset -> {\n      let reset = effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.reset_clock() })\n      case model.game_on {\n        False -> #(model, reset)\n        True -> {\n          let #(model, draw_effect) = game_redraw(Model(..model, ghosts: []))\n          #(model, effect.batch([reset, draw_effect]))\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    Play -> set_speed(model, 1.0)\n\n    Pause -> set_speed(model, 0.0)\n\n    ToggleGame ->\n      case model.game_on {\n        // The first time game mode is turned on, open the guide too (the level\n        // starts paused behind it).\n        False -> {\n          let first_time = !data.load_guide_seen()\n          let model = case first_time {\n            True -> Model(..model, guide: Some(0))\n            False -> model\n          }\n          let #(model, open_effect) = open_level(model, model.level)\n          case first_time {\n            False -> #(model, open_effect)\n            True -> #(\n              model,\n              effect.batch([\n                open_effect,\n                effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_guide_seen() }),\n              ]),\n            )\n          }\n        }\n        True -> {\n          let speed = model.speed\n          let #(model, open_effect) =\n            open_drawing(Model(..model, game_on: False), model.current)\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n                data.save_game_on(False)\n                render.clear_on_frame()\n                render.set_red_drawing([], False)\n                render.set_fixed([])\n                render.set_speed(speed)\n              }),\n              open_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    SelectLevel(name) -> open_level(model, name)\n\n    OpenGuide -> open_guide(model)\n\n    CloseGuide -> close_guide(model)\n\n    GuideNext -> #(\n      Model(\n        ..model,\n        guide: option.map(model.guide, fn(i) {\n          int.min(i + 1, tutorial.count() - 1)\n        }),\n      ),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    GuideBack -> #(\n      Model(\n        ..model,\n        guide: option.map(model.guide, fn(i) { int.max(i - 1, 0) }),\n      ),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    // Close the guide and start the Tutorial level (which restarts the clocks).\n    GuideToTutorial -> open_level(Model(..model, guide: None), \"Tutorial\")\n\n    // Start the level over: the anchor goes back to its start, old lines are\n    // cleared, and both clocks restart.\n    RestartLevel ->\n      case find_level(model.level) {\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n        Ok(level) -> {\n          let speed = model.speed\n          let guide_open = option.is_some(model.guide)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            game_redraw(\n              Model(..model, anchor: level.start, ghosts: [], status: Playing),\n            )\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n                start_clocks(level, speed, guide_open)\n              }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    GameEvent(event, t) ->\n      case model.game_on, model.status {\n        True, Playing ->\n          case event {\n            // The anchor moves for good. The lines drawn so far stay, frozen,\n            // and the player's clock starts over for lines from the new anchor.\n            game.AnchorReached(point) -> {\n              let ghosts =\n                list.append(model.ghosts, game.freeze(counting(model), t))\n              let #(model, draw_effect) =\n                game_redraw(Model(..model, anchor: point, ghosts: ghosts))\n              #(\n                model,\n                effect.batch([\n                  effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.reset_clock() }),\n                  draw_effect,\n                ]),\n              )\n            }\n            game.Won -> end_level(model, LevelWon)\n            game.Caught -> end_level(model, LevelLost(\"Caught!\"))\n            game.HitEdge -> end_level(model, LevelLost(\"Hit the edge!\"))\n          }\n        _, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n  }\n}\n\n// The list with key's value replaced by value.\nfn replace_key(\n  pairs: List(#(String, String)),\n  key: String,\n  value: String,\n) -> List(#(String, String)) {\n  list.map(pairs, fn(pair) {\n    case pair.0 == key {\n      True -> #(key, value)\n      False -> pair\n    }\n  })\n}\n\n// Show the named drawing: remember it as the open one, put its code in a fresh\n// editor, and animate it from the start.\nfn open_drawing(model: Model, name: String) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case list.key_find(model.drawings, name) {\n    Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n    Ok(source) -> #(\n      Model(..model, current: name, menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1),\n      effect.batch([\n        effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n          data.save_current(name)\n          render.reset_clock()\n        }),\n        editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n        // Mounting doesn't report the new text, so evaluate it explicitly.\n        effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n      ]),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// Record the new speed (so the slider follows) and apply it to the canvas. In\n// game mode, a negative speed also clears the player's old lines, and once a\n// level is over the clocks stay stopped until it restarts.\nfn set_speed(model: Model, rate: Float) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let model = Model(..model, speed: rate)\n  let apply = effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.set_speed(rate) })\n  case model.game_on, model.status {\n    False, _ -> #(model, apply)\n    True, Playing ->\n      case rate <. 0.0 && model.ghosts != [] {\n        False -> #(model, apply)\n        True -> {\n          let #(model, draw_effect) = game_redraw(Model(..model, ghosts: []))\n          #(model, effect.batch([apply, draw_effect]))\n        }\n      }\n    True, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n  }\n}\n\n// Evaluate the model's source at its shape-slider value. On success, send the\n// paths to the canvas; on failure, show the error message and draw nothing.\nfn redraw(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  use <- bool.lazy_guard(model.game_on, fn() { game_redraw(model) })\n  case run_source(model.shape_slider, model.source) {\n    Ok(paths) -> #(\n      Model(..model, output: \"\"),\n      effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n        paths\n        |> list.map(fn(p) { render.render_path(p, \"#cfe3ff\") })\n        |> render.set_drawing\n      }),\n    )\n    Error(msg) -> #(\n      Model(..model, output: msg),\n      effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.set_drawing([]) }),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Game mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfn find_level(name: String) -> Result(Level, Nil) {\n  list.find(levels.all(), fn(l) { l.name == name })\n}\n\n// Switch to game mode at the named level, from its start, with its saved code.\nfn open_level(model: Model, name: String) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case find_level(name), list.key_find(model.level_codes, name) {\n    Ok(level), Ok(source) -> {\n      let speed = model.speed\n      let guide_open = option.is_some(model.guide)\n      #(\n        Model(\n          ..model,\n          game_on: True,\n          level: name,\n          anchor: level.start,\n          target: None,\n          stage: [],\n          ghosts: [],\n          status: Playing,\n          output: \"\",\n          menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1,\n        ),\n        effect.batch([\n          effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n            data.save_game_on(True)\n            data.save_level(name)\n            start_clocks(level, speed, guide_open)\n          }),\n          editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n          // Mounting doesn't report the new text, so evaluate it explicitly.\n          effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n        ]),\n      )\n    }\n    _, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n  }\n}\n\n// Open the guide at its first step. Both clocks pause while it is open, so the\n// red lines can't catch the player while they read.\nfn open_guide(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  #(\n    Model(..model, guide: Some(0)),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      data.save_guide_seen()\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// Close the guide and, if the level is still being played, resume both clocks.\nfn close_guide(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let speed = model.speed\n  let resume = model.game_on && model.status == Playing\n  #(\n    Model(..model, guide: None),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      case resume {\n        True -> {\n          render.set_speed(speed)\n          render.set_red_speed(1.0)\n        }\n        False -> Nil\n      }\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// Show the level's red paths and put both clocks back at the start, running\n// unless the guide is open. (Lustre doesn't promise the order of batched effects,\n// so this decides about the guide itself rather than relying on a later pause.)\nfn start_clocks(level: Level, speed: Float, guide_open: Bool) -> Nil {\n  render.set_red_drawing(\n    list.map(level.red, render.render_path(_, red_color)),\n    level.loop,\n  )\n  render.reset_red_clock()\n  render.reset_clock()\n  case guide_open {\n    True -> {\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n    }\n    False -> {\n      render.set_red_speed(1.0)\n      render.set_speed(speed)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// The player's lines that count: those starting at the anchor.\nfn counting(model: Model) -> List(Path) {\n  list.filter(model.stage, game.counts(_, model.anchor))\n}\n\n// Run the level's code at the current anchor and show the result: the player's\n// lines, the fixed layer, and (while playing) a per-frame check for events.\nfn game_redraw(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let model = case game.run(model.source, model.shape_slider, model.anchor) {\n    Ok(#(paths, target)) ->\n      Model(..model, stage: paths, target: target, output: \"\")\n    Error(msg) -> Model(..model, stage: [], target: None, output: msg)\n  }\n  #(model, effect.from(fn(dispatch) { show_game(model, dispatch) }))\n}\n\nfn show_game(model: Model, dispatch: fn(Msg) -> Nil) -> Nil {\n  render.set_drawing(\n    list.map(model.stage, fn(p) {\n      render.render_path(p, case game.counts(p, model.anchor) {\n        True -> player_color\n        False -> faded_color\n      })\n    }),\n  )\n  render.set_fixed(fixed_layer(model))\n  case model.status, find_level(model.level) {\n    Playing, Ok(level) -> {\n      let snapshot =\n        game.Snapshot(\n          anchor: model.anchor,\n          target: model.target,\n          lines: counting(model),\n          ghosts: model.ghosts,\n          red: level.red,\n        )\n      render.on_frame(fn(t, red_t) {\n        case game.check(snapshot, t, red_t) {\n          None -> False\n          Some(event) -> {\n            dispatch(GameEvent(event, t))\n            True\n          }\n        }\n      })\n    }\n    _, _ -> render.clear_on_frame()\n  }\n}\n\n// The level is over: stop both clocks and the per-frame check.\nfn end_level(model: Model, status: Status) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  #(\n    Model(..model, status: status),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      render.clear_on_frame()\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// What is always drawn in game mode: the goal line along the top, the player's\n// old lines, the next anchor position (if any), and the anchor itself.\nfn fixed_layer(model: Model) -> List(render.RenderPath) {\n  let goal =\n    render.render_path(\n      instant(\n        Point(-500.0, game.top +. 3.0),\n        Point(500.0, game.top +. 3.0),\n        6.0,\n      ),\n      goal_color,\n    )\n  let ghosts = list.map(model.ghosts, render.render_path(_, ghost_color))\n  let target = case model.target {\n    Some(p) -> marker(p, 8.0, 2.0, target_color)\n    None -> []\n  }\n  list.flatten([\n    [goal],\n    ghosts,\n    target,\n    marker(model.anchor, 12.0, 3.0, anchor_color),\n  ])\n}\n\n// A diamond of radius r around p.\nfn marker(\n  p: Point,\n  r: Float,\n  thickness: Float,\n  color: String,\n) -> List(render.RenderPath) {\n  let n = Point(p.x, p.y -. r)\n  let e = Point(p.x +. r, p.y)\n  let s = Point(p.x, p.y +. r)\n  let w = Point(p.x -. r, p.y)\n  list.map([#(n, e), #(e, s), #(s, w), #(w, n)], fn(side) {\n    render.render_path(instant(side.0, side.1, thickness), color)\n  })\n}\n\n// A line that is drawn complete at once.\nfn instant(a: Point, b: Point, thickness: Float) -> Path {\n  Path(Line(a, b), 0.0, 0.0, thickness)\n}\n\n// Range inputs report whole numbers without a decimal point (\"1\", \"-2\"), which\n// float.parse rejects, so fall back to parsing an Int.\nfn parse_number(text: String) -> Result(Float, Nil) {\n  case float.parse(text) {\n    Ok(x) -> Ok(x)\n    Error(_) -> int.parse(text) |> result.map(int.to_float)\n  }\n}\n\n// ── View ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Two side-by-side panes: the code editor on the left and its rendered output on\n// the right. The code column can be hidden to expand the right pane.\n\npub fn view(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let app_class = case model.code_open {\n    True -> \"app\"\n    False -> \"app code-hidden\"\n  }\n  // The code column still exists even when hidden (CSS collapses it) so the\n  // mounted CodeMirror editor keeps its state across show/hide.\n  html.div([attr.class(app_class)], [\n    html.div([attr.class(\"code-col\")], [\n      editor.view_panel(ToggleCode, view_library(model)),\n    ]),\n    view_anim(model),\n    view_guide(model),\n  ])\n}\n\n// The \"How to play\" guide, over the whole app while it is open in game mode.\nfn view_guide(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.game_on, model.guide {\n    True, Some(step) ->\n      tutorial.view(\n        step,\n        on_back: GuideBack,\n        on_next: GuideNext,\n        on_close: CloseGuide,\n        to_tutorial: GuideToTutorial,\n      )\n    _, _ -> element.none()\n  }\n}\n\n// The strip above the code: the drawing menu (every saved drawing, then\n// \"+ New drawing…\"), or in game mode the level menu, a restart button, and a\n// button that opens the \"How to play\" guide. A menu is keyed by menu_revision\n// so that bumping it rebuilds the <select> showing the open drawing or level.\nfn view_library(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let revision = int.to_string(model.menu_revision)\n  case model.game_on {\n    False ->\n      keyed.div([attr.class(\"library\")], [\n        #(\"drawings-\" <> revision, view_drawing_menu(model)),\n      ])\n    True ->\n      keyed.div([attr.class(\"library\")], [\n        #(\"levels-\" <> revision, view_level_menu(model)),\n        #(\n          \"restart\",\n          html.button(\n            [\n              attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n              attr.title(\"Restart level\"),\n              event.on_click(RestartLevel),\n            ],\n            [html.text(\"Restart\")],\n          ),\n        ),\n        #(\n          \"guide\",\n          html.button(\n            [\n              attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n              attr.title(\"How to play\"),\n              event.on_click(OpenGuide),\n            ],\n            [html.text(\"How to play\")],\n          ),\n        ),\n      ])\n  }\n}\n\nfn view_drawing_menu(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let options =\n    list.map(model.drawings, fn(d) {\n      html.option([attr.value(d.0), attr.selected(d.0 == model.current)], d.0)\n    })\n  html.select(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"library-select\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Drawing\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"drawing-select\"),\n      event.on_change(SelectDrawing),\n    ],\n    list.append(options, [\n      html.option([attr.value(new_drawing_option)], \"+ New drawing…\"),\n    ]),\n  )\n}\n\nfn view_level_menu(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.select(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"library-select\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Level\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"level-select\"),\n      event.on_change(SelectLevel),\n    ],\n    list.map(levels.all(), fn(l) {\n      html.option(\n        [attr.value(l.name), attr.selected(l.name == model.level)],\n        l.name,\n      )\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// The game-mode toggle, in the top-right corner of the animation pane.\nfn view_game_toggle(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.button(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"game-toggle\"),\n      attr.title(case model.game_on {\n        True -> \"Back to drawing\"\n        False -> \"Play the game\"\n      }),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-pressed\", case model.game_on {\n        True -> \"true\"\n        False -> \"false\"\n      }),\n      event.on_click(ToggleGame),\n    ],\n    [\n      html.text(\"Problem 12: Game\"),\n    ],\n  )\n}\n\n// The end-of-level banner, over the canvas.\nfn view_banner(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.game_on, model.status {\n    True, LevelWon -> banner(\"Level complete!\", \"banner won\")\n    True, LevelLost(reason) -> banner(reason, \"banner lost\")\n    _, _ -> element.none()\n  }\n}\n\nfn banner(title: String, class: String) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.div([attr.class(class)], [\n    html.div([attr.class(\"banner-title\")], [html.text(title)]),\n    html.button([attr.class(\"library-btn\"), event.on_click(RestartLevel)], [\n      html.text(\"Restart level\"),\n    ]),\n  ])\n}\n\nfn view_anim(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.div([attr.class(\"canvas-area\")], [\n    view_reveal_code(model),\n    html.canvas([attr.id(\"animation-canvas\"), attr.class(\"canvas\")]),\n    html.pre([attr.class(\"output\")], [html.text(model.output)]),\n    view_banner(model),\n    view_game_toggle(model),\n    view_controls(model),\n  ])\n}\n\n// The playback controls, in a small box overlaid on the bottom-right corner of\n// the animation pane: a collapse toggle at the top right, then the reset / play /\n// pause buttons with the speed slider, then the shape slider. Collapsing only\n// hides the rows (via CSS), so the sliders stay in the page.\nfn view_controls(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let #(box_class, toggle_icon, toggle_title) = case model.controls_open {\n    True -> #(\"controls\", \"▾\", \"Hide controls\")\n    False -> #(\"controls collapsed\", \"▸\", \"Show controls\")\n  }\n  html.div([attr.class(box_class)], [\n    html.button(\n      [\n        attr.class(\"controls-toggle\"),\n        attr.title(toggle_title),\n        attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", toggle_title),\n        attr.attribute(\"aria-expanded\", case model.controls_open {\n          True -> \"true\"\n          False -> \"false\"\n        }),\n        event.on_click(ToggleControls),\n      ],\n      [html.text(toggle_icon)],\n    ),\n    html.div([attr.class(\"control\")], [\n      html.div([attr.class(\"playback-buttons\")], [\n        playback_button(\"⟲\", \"Reset\", Reset, \"reset-btn\"),\n        playback_button(\"▶\\u{FE0E}\", \"Play\", Play, \"\"),\n        playback_button(\"❚❚\", \"Pause\", Pause, \"\"),\n      ]),\n      html.input([\n        attr.type_(\"range\"),\n        attr.min(float.to_string(speed_min)),\n        attr.max(float.to_string(speed_max)),\n        attr.step(\"0.1\"),\n        attr.value(float.to_string(model.speed)),\n        attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"speed-slider\"),\n        event.on_input(SetSpeed),\n      ]),\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-value\")], [\n        html.text(float.to_string(float.to_precision(model.speed, 1)) <> \"×\"),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    html.label([attr.class(\"control\")], [\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-label\")], [html.text(\"shape_slider\")]),\n      html.input([\n        attr.type_(\"range\"),\n        attr.min(int.to_string(shape_slider_min)),\n        attr.max(int.to_string(shape_slider_max)),\n        attr.step(\"1\"),\n        attr.value(int.to_string(model.shape_slider)),\n        attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"shape-slider\"),\n        event.on_input(SetShapeSlider),\n      ]),\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-value\")], [\n        html.text(int.to_string(model.shape_slider)),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n  ])\n}\n\nfn playback_button(\n  icon: String,\n  label: String,\n  msg: Msg,\n  extra_class: String,\n) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.button(\n    [\n      attr.class(string.trim(\"playback-btn \" <> extra_class)),\n      attr.title(label),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", label),\n      event.on_click(msg),\n    ],\n    [html.text(icon)],\n  )\n}\n\n// The button that brings the code column back, overlaid on the top-left of the\n// right pane. Nothing is rendered while the column is already showing (its own\n// header holds the button that hides it).\nfn view_reveal_code(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.code_open {\n    True -> element.none()\n    False ->\n      html.button(\n        [\n          attr.class(\"icon-btn reveal-code\"),\n          attr.attribute(\"title\", \"Show code\"),\n          event.on_click(ToggleCode),\n        ],\n        [html.text(\"⟩\")],\n      )\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Main ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn main() -> Nil {\n  let app = lustre.application(init, update, view)\n  let assert Ok(_) = lustre.start(app, \"#app\", Nil)\n  Nil\n}\n","new_content_size":31647,"new_hash":"a5aefe8426b92e2f0dc3a7dbcfd388b1d39cf2b18aa23131b33f7935e0a1d217","old_hash":"e92efc2d407efc51779c71b4dda58ce45cc0fbb56620b20c11cc033c9b2a2964","operation":"modify","path":"src/app.gleam"},"hash":"5b4af96e385f2b04771d92c28606889f82b4dbda87379bf49c78dfd86ffa5319","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"843dc727c0efaa697abac25fc07da934e0ec46b3417d6a7972181d667d4c2bcd","seq":5247,"t":77596499,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:34.034Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":199,"new_content":"//// Animator -- an application for program-generated drawings.\n\nimport builtins\nimport data\nimport drawing\nimport editor\nimport errors\nimport game\nimport gleam/bool\nimport gleam/float\nimport gleam/int\nimport gleam/list\nimport gleam/option.{type Option, None, Some}\nimport gleam/result\nimport gleam/string\nimport levels.{type Level}\nimport lustre\nimport lustre/attribute as attr\nimport lustre/effect.{type Effect}\nimport lustre/element.{type Element}\nimport lustre/element/html\nimport lustre/element/keyed\nimport lustre/event\nimport parser\nimport render\nimport shape.{type Path, type Point, Line, Path, Point}\nimport tutorial\n\n/// Your app's public address, once you've deployed it in Problem 13, such as\n/// \"https://your-username.github.io/animator/\".\npub const public_url = \"\"\n\n/// Set to True if you would like your app's URL included in the course gallery.\npub const include_app_in_gallery = False\n\n/// A link to your video from Problem 14, which anyone with the link can watch.\npub const video_url = \"\"\n\n/// Set to True if you would like your video included in the course gallery.\npub const include_video_in_gallery = False\n\n// Range and starting value of the shape slider.\nconst shape_slider_min = 0\n\nconst shape_slider_max = 100\n\nconst default_shape_slider = 10\n\n// Range of the speed slider, as a multiple of normal speed.\nconst speed_min = -6.0\n\nconst speed_max = 6.0\n\n// The menu value of the \"+ New drawing…\" option. Drawing names are never empty,\n// so it can't collide with one.\nconst new_drawing_option = \"\"\n\n// The code a new drawing starts with.\nconst new_drawing_code =\n  \"fn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\\n  [Path(Line(Point(0, 0), Point(100, 100)), 0, 10, 2)]\\n}\\n\"\n\n// Colors on the canvas.\nconst player_color = \"#cfe3ff\"\n\n// Game mode: player lines that don't start at the anchor (and so don't count),\n// old lines left behind by an anchor move, the red paths, the anchor marker and\n// the next anchor position, and the goal line along the top edge.\nconst faded_color = \"#4a5570\"\n\nconst ghost_color = \"#8a9cc0\"\n\nconst red_color = \"#ff4d4d\"\n\nconst anchor_color = \"#ffcb6b\"\n\nconst target_color = \"#8a7440\"\n\nconst goal_color = \"#4caf7a\"\n\n// ── Model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub type Model {\n  Model(\n    // The code in the editor.\n    source: String,\n    // The library: every drawing's #(name, source), in menu order.\n    drawings: List(#(String, String)),\n    // The name of the drawing open in the editor.\n    current: String,\n    // Bumped to rebuild the drawing menu, so it shows `current` again after\n    // the \"+ New drawing…\" option was picked (e.g. when the prompt is cancelled).\n    menu_revision: Int,\n    // Whether the code editor is shown.\n    code_open: Bool,\n    output: String,\n    // The value passed to draw as shape_slider.\n    shape_slider: Int,\n    // Animation speed: 1.0 is normal, 0.0 pauses, negative plays backward.\n    speed: Float,\n    // Whether the playback-controls box is expanded.\n    controls_open: Bool,\n    // ── Game mode (see game.gleam and extension.txt) ──\n    game_on: Bool,\n    // Every level's code, #(level name, source), in menu order.\n    level_codes: List(#(String, String)),\n    // The name of the level being played.\n    level: String,\n    // Where the player's lines must start.\n    anchor: Point,\n    // Where move_anchor says the anchor goes next, if anywhere.\n    target: Option(Point),\n    // The paths the player's draw returned for the current anchor.\n    stage: List(Path),\n    // The player's old lines, frozen when the anchor moved, until cleared.\n    ghosts: List(Path),\n    status: Status,\n    // The \"How to play\" guide's open step (counting from 0), if it is open.\n    guide: Option(Int),\n  )\n}\n\n/// How the current attempt at a level stands.\npub type Status {\n  Playing\n  LevelWon\n  // Lost, with the banner's title saying why.\n  LevelLost(reason: String)\n}\n\npub type Msg {\n  SetCode(String)\n\n  // Drawing library\n  SelectDrawing(String)\n  CreateDrawing(String)\n\n  // Sliders (the browser reports a range input's value as a string)\n  SetShapeSlider(String)\n  SetSpeed(String)\n\n  // Playback buttons\n  Reset\n  Play\n  Pause\n\n  // Layout controls\n  ToggleCode\n  ToggleControls\n\n  // Game mode\n  ToggleGame\n  SelectLevel(String)\n  RestartLevel\n  // Something happened in play, at this main-clock time.\n  GameEvent(game.Event, Float)\n\n  // The \"How to play\" guide\n  OpenGuide\n  CloseGuide\n  GuideNext\n  GuideBack\n  GuideToTutorial\n}\n\n// ── Init ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn init(_flags: Nil) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  // Reopen the saved library at the drawing that was open last (or the first).\n  let drawings = data.load_library()\n  let first = case drawings {\n    [#(name, _), ..] -> name\n    [] -> \"\"\n  }\n  let current = case data.load_current() {\n    Ok(name) ->\n      case list.key_find(drawings, name) {\n        Ok(_) -> name\n        Error(_) -> first\n      }\n    Error(_) -> first\n  }\n  let source = list.key_find(drawings, current) |> result.unwrap(\"\")\n  // Each level's saved code (or the starter code), and the level open last.\n  let level_codes =\n    list.map(levels.all(), fn(l) {\n      #(l.name, data.load_level_code(l.name) |> result.unwrap(l.starter))\n    })\n  let first_level = case level_codes {\n    [#(name, _), ..] -> name\n    [] -> \"\"\n  }\n  let level = case data.load_level() {\n    Ok(name) ->\n      case list.key_find(level_codes, name) {\n        Ok(_) -> name\n        Error(_) -> first_level\n      }\n    Error(_) -> first_level\n  }\n  let model =\n    Model(\n      source: source,\n      drawings: drawings,\n      current: current,\n      menu_revision: 0,\n      code_open: True,\n      output: \"\",\n      shape_slider: default_shape_slider,\n      speed: 1.0,\n      controls_open: True,\n      game_on: False,\n      level_codes: level_codes,\n      level: level,\n      anchor: Point(0.0, 0.0),\n      target: None,\n      stage: [],\n      ghosts: [],\n      status: Playing,\n      guide: None,\n    )\n  // Reopen game mode if it was on when the page was last used.\n  case data.load_game_on() {\n    True -> open_level(model, level)\n    False -> #(\n      model,\n      // Effects that happen automatically at the start go here.\n      effect.batch([\n        editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n        effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n      ]),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// Parse and evaluate a draw function at the given shape-slider value, returning\n// the rendered paths on success, or a human-readable message on failure.\nfn run_source(shape_slider: Int, source: String) -> Result(List(Path), String) {\n  case parser.parse_program(source, builtins.global_frame()) {\n    Error(msg) -> Error(msg)\n    Ok(program) ->\n      drawing.try_draw(shape_slider, program)\n      // Convert Error(EvalError) to Error(String) with a useful error message.\n      |> result.map_error(errors.error_message)\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Update ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn update(model: Model, msg: Msg) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case msg {\n    ToggleCode -> #(Model(..model, code_open: !model.code_open), effect.none())\n\n    ToggleControls -> #(\n      Model(..model, controls_open: !model.controls_open),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    // After each keystroke in the editor, save the code into the open drawing,\n    // then evaluate it and report errors.\n    // In game mode, the code is saved as the open level's instead.\n    SetCode(text) ->\n      case model.game_on {\n        False -> {\n          let drawings = replace_key(model.drawings, model.current, text)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            redraw(Model(..model, source: text, drawings: drawings))\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_library(drawings) }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n        True -> {\n          let level = model.level\n          let level_codes = replace_key(model.level_codes, level, text)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            redraw(Model(..model, source: text, level_codes: level_codes))\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_level_code(level, text) }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    // Picking \"+ New drawing…\" asks for a name; any other option opens that\n    // drawing.\n    SelectDrawing(value) ->\n      case value == new_drawing_option {\n        True -> #(\n          model,\n          effect.from(fn(dispatch) {\n            dispatch(CreateDrawing(data.prompt_drawing_name()))\n          }),\n        )\n        False -> open_drawing(model, value)\n      }\n\n    // A blank name (or a cancelled prompt) only puts the menu back; an existing\n    // name opens that drawing; a new name adds a drawing and opens it.\n    CreateDrawing(name) -> {\n      let name = string.trim(name)\n      case name, list.key_find(model.drawings, name) {\n        \"\", _ -> #(\n          Model(..model, menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1),\n          effect.none(),\n        )\n        _, Ok(_) -> open_drawing(model, name)\n        _, Error(_) -> {\n          let drawings =\n            list.append(model.drawings, [#(name, new_drawing_code)])\n          let #(model, open_effect) =\n            open_drawing(Model(..model, drawings: drawings), name)\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_library(drawings) }),\n              open_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Moving the shape slider re-runs draw on the new value.\n    SetShapeSlider(value) ->\n      case int.parse(value) {\n        Ok(n) -> redraw(Model(..model, shape_slider: n))\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n\n    SetSpeed(value) ->\n      case parse_number(value) {\n        Ok(rate) -> set_speed(model, rate)\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n\n    // Jump back to the start (empty canvas); playback continues at the\n    // current speed.\n    // In game mode this also clears the player's old lines.\n    Reset -> {\n      let reset = effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.reset_clock() })\n      case model.game_on {\n        False -> #(model, reset)\n        True -> {\n          let #(model, draw_effect) = game_redraw(Model(..model, ghosts: []))\n          #(model, effect.batch([reset, draw_effect]))\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    Play -> set_speed(model, 1.0)\n\n    Pause -> set_speed(model, 0.0)\n\n    ToggleGame ->\n      case model.game_on {\n        // The first time game mode is turned on, open the guide too (the level\n        // starts paused behind it).\n        False -> {\n          let first_time = !data.load_guide_seen()\n          let model = case first_time {\n            True -> Model(..model, guide: Some(0))\n            False -> model\n          }\n          let #(model, open_effect) = open_level(model, model.level)\n          case first_time {\n            False -> #(model, open_effect)\n            True -> #(\n              model,\n              effect.batch([\n                open_effect,\n                effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { data.save_guide_seen() }),\n              ]),\n            )\n          }\n        }\n        True -> {\n          let speed = model.speed\n          let #(model, open_effect) =\n            open_drawing(Model(..model, game_on: False), model.current)\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n                data.save_game_on(False)\n                render.clear_on_frame()\n                render.set_red_drawing([], False)\n                render.set_fixed([])\n                render.set_speed(speed)\n              }),\n              open_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    SelectLevel(name) -> open_level(model, name)\n\n    OpenGuide -> open_guide(model)\n\n    CloseGuide -> close_guide(model)\n\n    GuideNext -> #(\n      Model(\n        ..model,\n        guide: option.map(model.guide, fn(i) {\n          int.min(i + 1, tutorial.count() - 1)\n        }),\n      ),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    GuideBack -> #(\n      Model(\n        ..model,\n        guide: option.map(model.guide, fn(i) { int.max(i - 1, 0) }),\n      ),\n      effect.none(),\n    )\n\n    // Close the guide and start the Tutorial level (which restarts the clocks).\n    GuideToTutorial -> open_level(Model(..model, guide: None), \"Tutorial\")\n\n    // Start the level over: the anchor goes back to its start, old lines are\n    // cleared, and both clocks restart.\n    RestartLevel ->\n      case find_level(model.level) {\n        Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n        Ok(level) -> {\n          let speed = model.speed\n          let guide_open = option.is_some(model.guide)\n          let #(model, draw_effect) =\n            game_redraw(\n              Model(..model, anchor: level.start, ghosts: [], status: Playing),\n            )\n          #(\n            model,\n            effect.batch([\n              effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n                start_clocks(level, speed, guide_open)\n              }),\n              draw_effect,\n            ]),\n          )\n        }\n      }\n\n    GameEvent(event, t) ->\n      case model.game_on, model.status {\n        True, Playing ->\n          case event {\n            // The anchor moves for good. The lines drawn so far stay, frozen,\n            // and the player's clock starts over for lines from the new anchor.\n            game.AnchorReached(point) -> {\n              let ghosts =\n                list.append(model.ghosts, game.freeze(counting(model), t))\n              let #(model, draw_effect) =\n                game_redraw(Model(..model, anchor: point, ghosts: ghosts))\n              #(\n                model,\n                effect.batch([\n                  effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.reset_clock() }),\n                  draw_effect,\n                ]),\n              )\n            }\n            game.Won -> end_level(model, LevelWon)\n            game.Caught -> end_level(model, LevelLost(\"Caught!\"))\n            game.HitEdge -> end_level(model, LevelLost(\"Hit the edge!\"))\n          }\n        _, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n      }\n  }\n}\n\n// The list with key's value replaced by value.\nfn replace_key(\n  pairs: List(#(String, String)),\n  key: String,\n  value: String,\n) -> List(#(String, String)) {\n  list.map(pairs, fn(pair) {\n    case pair.0 == key {\n      True -> #(key, value)\n      False -> pair\n    }\n  })\n}\n\n// Show the named drawing: remember it as the open one, put its code in a fresh\n// editor, and animate it from the start.\nfn open_drawing(model: Model, name: String) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case list.key_find(model.drawings, name) {\n    Error(_) -> #(model, effect.none())\n    Ok(source) -> #(\n      Model(..model, current: name, menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1),\n      effect.batch([\n        effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n          data.save_current(name)\n          render.reset_clock()\n        }),\n        editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n        // Mounting doesn't report the new text, so evaluate it explicitly.\n        effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n      ]),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// Record the new speed (so the slider follows) and apply it to the canvas. In\n// game mode, a negative speed also clears the player's old lines, and once a\n// level is over the clocks stay stopped until it restarts.\nfn set_speed(model: Model, rate: Float) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let model = Model(..model, speed: rate)\n  let apply = effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.set_speed(rate) })\n  case model.game_on, model.status {\n    False, _ -> #(model, apply)\n    True, Playing ->\n      case rate <. 0.0 && model.ghosts != [] {\n        False -> #(model, apply)\n        True -> {\n          let #(model, draw_effect) = game_redraw(Model(..model, ghosts: []))\n          #(model, effect.batch([apply, draw_effect]))\n        }\n      }\n    True, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n  }\n}\n\n// Evaluate the model's source at its shape-slider value. On success, send the\n// paths to the canvas; on failure, show the error message and draw nothing.\nfn redraw(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  use <- bool.lazy_guard(model.game_on, fn() { game_redraw(model) })\n  case run_source(model.shape_slider, model.source) {\n    Ok(paths) -> #(\n      Model(..model, output: \"\"),\n      effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n        paths\n        |> list.map(fn(p) { render.render_path(p, \"#cfe3ff\") })\n        |> render.set_drawing\n      }),\n    )\n    Error(msg) -> #(\n      Model(..model, output: msg),\n      effect.from(fn(_dispatch) { render.set_drawing([]) }),\n    )\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Game mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfn find_level(name: String) -> Result(Level, Nil) {\n  list.find(levels.all(), fn(l) { l.name == name })\n}\n\n// Switch to game mode at the named level, from its start, with its saved code.\nfn open_level(model: Model, name: String) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  case find_level(name), list.key_find(model.level_codes, name) {\n    Ok(level), Ok(source) -> {\n      let speed = model.speed\n      let guide_open = option.is_some(model.guide)\n      #(\n        Model(\n          ..model,\n          game_on: True,\n          level: name,\n          anchor: level.start,\n          target: None,\n          stage: [],\n          ghosts: [],\n          status: Playing,\n          output: \"\",\n          menu_revision: model.menu_revision + 1,\n        ),\n        effect.batch([\n          effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n            data.save_game_on(True)\n            data.save_level(name)\n            start_clocks(level, speed, guide_open)\n          }),\n          editor.mount(source, SetCode),\n          // Mounting doesn't report the new text, so evaluate it explicitly.\n          effect.from(fn(dispatch) { dispatch(SetCode(source)) }),\n        ]),\n      )\n    }\n    _, _ -> #(model, effect.none())\n  }\n}\n\n// Open the guide at its first step. Both clocks pause while it is open, so the\n// red lines can't catch the player while they read.\nfn open_guide(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  #(\n    Model(..model, guide: Some(0)),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      data.save_guide_seen()\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// Close the guide and, if the level is still being played, resume both clocks.\nfn close_guide(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let speed = model.speed\n  let resume = model.game_on && model.status == Playing\n  #(\n    Model(..model, guide: None),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      case resume {\n        True -> {\n          render.set_speed(speed)\n          render.set_red_speed(1.0)\n        }\n        False -> Nil\n      }\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// Show the level's red paths and put both clocks back at the start, running\n// unless the guide is open. (Lustre doesn't promise the order of batched effects,\n// so this decides about the guide itself rather than relying on a later pause.)\nfn start_clocks(level: Level, speed: Float, guide_open: Bool) -> Nil {\n  render.set_red_drawing(\n    list.map(level.red, render.render_path(_, red_color)),\n    level.loop,\n  )\n  render.reset_red_clock()\n  render.reset_clock()\n  case guide_open {\n    True -> {\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n    }\n    False -> {\n      render.set_red_speed(1.0)\n      render.set_speed(speed)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// The player's lines that count: those starting at the anchor.\nfn counting(model: Model) -> List(Path) {\n  list.filter(model.stage, game.counts(_, model.anchor))\n}\n\n// Run the level's code at the current anchor and show the result: the player's\n// lines, the fixed layer, and (while playing) a per-frame check for events.\nfn game_redraw(model: Model) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  let model = case game.run(model.source, model.shape_slider, model.anchor) {\n    Ok(#(paths, target)) ->\n      Model(..model, stage: paths, target: target, output: \"\")\n    Error(msg) -> Model(..model, stage: [], target: None, output: msg)\n  }\n  #(model, effect.from(fn(dispatch) { show_game(model, dispatch) }))\n}\n\nfn show_game(model: Model, dispatch: fn(Msg) -> Nil) -> Nil {\n  render.set_drawing(\n    list.map(model.stage, fn(p) {\n      render.render_path(p, case game.counts(p, model.anchor) {\n        True -> player_color\n        False -> faded_color\n      })\n    }),\n  )\n  render.set_fixed(fixed_layer(model))\n  case model.status, find_level(model.level) {\n    Playing, Ok(level) -> {\n      let snapshot =\n        game.Snapshot(\n          anchor: model.anchor,\n          target: model.target,\n          lines: counting(model),\n          ghosts: model.ghosts,\n          red: level.red,\n        )\n      render.on_frame(fn(t, red_t) {\n        case game.check(snapshot, t, red_t) {\n          None -> False\n          Some(event) -> {\n            dispatch(GameEvent(event, t))\n            True\n          }\n        }\n      })\n    }\n    _, _ -> render.clear_on_frame()\n  }\n}\n\n// The level is over: stop both clocks and the per-frame check.\nfn end_level(model: Model, status: Status) -> #(Model, Effect(Msg)) {\n  #(\n    Model(..model, status: status),\n    effect.from(fn(_dispatch) {\n      render.clear_on_frame()\n      render.set_speed(0.0)\n      render.set_red_speed(0.0)\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// What is always drawn in game mode: the goal bar along the top, the player's\n// old lines, the next anchor position (if any), and the anchor itself.\nfn fixed_layer(model: Model) -> List(render.RenderPath) {\n  // The bar is one line across the canvas, as thick as the bar is tall, so it\n  // fills everything from the top edge down to game.goal.\n  let middle = { game.top +. game.goal } /. 2.0\n  let goal =\n    render.render_path(\n      instant(\n        Point(0.0 -. game.edge, middle),\n        Point(game.edge, middle),\n        game.goal -. game.top,\n      ),\n      goal_color,\n    )\n  let ghosts = list.map(model.ghosts, render.render_path(_, ghost_color))\n  let target = case model.target {\n    Some(p) -> marker(p, 8.0, 2.0, target_color)\n    None -> []\n  }\n  list.flatten([\n    [goal],\n    ghosts,\n    target,\n    marker(model.anchor, 12.0, 3.0, anchor_color),\n  ])\n}\n\n// A diamond of radius r around p.\nfn marker(\n  p: Point,\n  r: Float,\n  thickness: Float,\n  color: String,\n) -> List(render.RenderPath) {\n  let n = Point(p.x, p.y -. r)\n  let e = Point(p.x +. r, p.y)\n  let s = Point(p.x, p.y +. r)\n  let w = Point(p.x -. r, p.y)\n  list.map([#(n, e), #(e, s), #(s, w), #(w, n)], fn(side) {\n    render.render_path(instant(side.0, side.1, thickness), color)\n  })\n}\n\n// A line that is drawn complete at once.\nfn instant(a: Point, b: Point, thickness: Float) -> Path {\n  Path(Line(a, b), 0.0, 0.0, thickness)\n}\n\n// Range inputs report whole numbers without a decimal point (\"1\", \"-2\"), which\n// float.parse rejects, so fall back to parsing an Int.\nfn parse_number(text: String) -> Result(Float, Nil) {\n  case float.parse(text) {\n    Ok(x) -> Ok(x)\n    Error(_) -> int.parse(text) |> result.map(int.to_float)\n  }\n}\n\n// ── View ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Two side-by-side panes: the code editor on the left and its rendered output on\n// the right. The code column can be hidden to expand the right pane.\n\npub fn view(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let app_class = case model.code_open {\n    True -> \"app\"\n    False -> \"app code-hidden\"\n  }\n  // The code column still exists even when hidden (CSS collapses it) so the\n  // mounted CodeMirror editor keeps its state across show/hide.\n  html.div([attr.class(app_class)], [\n    html.div([attr.class(\"code-col\")], [\n      editor.view_panel(ToggleCode, view_library(model)),\n    ]),\n    view_anim(model),\n    view_guide(model),\n  ])\n}\n\n// The \"How to play\" guide, over the whole app while it is open in game mode.\nfn view_guide(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.game_on, model.guide {\n    True, Some(step) ->\n      tutorial.view(\n        step,\n        on_back: GuideBack,\n        on_next: GuideNext,\n        on_close: CloseGuide,\n        to_tutorial: GuideToTutorial,\n      )\n    _, _ -> element.none()\n  }\n}\n\n// The strip above the code: the drawing menu (every saved drawing, then\n// \"+ New drawing…\"), or in game mode the level menu, a restart button, and a\n// button that opens the \"How to play\" guide. A menu is keyed by menu_revision\n// so that bumping it rebuilds the <select> showing the open drawing or level.\nfn view_library(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let revision = int.to_string(model.menu_revision)\n  case model.game_on {\n    False ->\n      keyed.div([attr.class(\"library\")], [\n        #(\"drawings-\" <> revision, view_drawing_menu(model)),\n      ])\n    True ->\n      keyed.div([attr.class(\"library\")], [\n        #(\"levels-\" <> revision, view_level_menu(model)),\n        #(\n          \"restart\",\n          html.button(\n            [\n              attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n              attr.title(\"Restart level\"),\n              event.on_click(RestartLevel),\n            ],\n            [html.text(\"Restart\")],\n          ),\n        ),\n        #(\n          \"guide\",\n          html.button(\n            [\n              attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n              attr.title(\"How to play\"),\n              event.on_click(OpenGuide),\n            ],\n            [html.text(\"How to play\")],\n          ),\n        ),\n      ])\n  }\n}\n\nfn view_drawing_menu(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let options =\n    list.map(model.drawings, fn(d) {\n      html.option([attr.value(d.0), attr.selected(d.0 == model.current)], d.0)\n    })\n  html.select(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"library-select\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Drawing\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"drawing-select\"),\n      event.on_change(SelectDrawing),\n    ],\n    list.append(options, [\n      html.option([attr.value(new_drawing_option)], \"+ New drawing…\"),\n    ]),\n  )\n}\n\nfn view_level_menu(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.select(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"library-select\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Level\"),\n      attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"level-select\"),\n      event.on_change(SelectLevel),\n    ],\n    list.map(levels.all(), fn(l) {\n      html.option(\n        [attr.value(l.name), attr.selected(l.name == model.level)],\n        l.name,\n      )\n    }),\n  )\n}\n\n// The game-mode toggle, in the top-right corner of the animation pane.\nfn view_game_toggle(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.button(\n    [\n      attr.class(\"game-toggle\"),\n      attr.title(case model.game_on {\n        True -> \"Back to drawing\"\n        False -> \"Play the game\"\n      }),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-pressed\", case model.game_on {\n        True -> \"true\"\n        False -> \"false\"\n      }),\n      event.on_click(ToggleGame),\n    ],\n    [\n      html.text(\"Problem 12: Game\"),\n    ],\n  )\n}\n\n// The end-of-level banner, over the canvas.\nfn view_banner(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.game_on, model.status {\n    True, LevelWon -> banner(\"Level complete!\", \"banner won\")\n    True, LevelLost(reason) -> banner(reason, \"banner lost\")\n    _, _ -> element.none()\n  }\n}\n\nfn banner(title: String, class: String) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.div([attr.class(class)], [\n    html.div([attr.class(\"banner-title\")], [html.text(title)]),\n    html.button([attr.class(\"library-btn\"), event.on_click(RestartLevel)], [\n      html.text(\"Restart level\"),\n    ]),\n  ])\n}\n\nfn view_anim(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.div([attr.class(\"canvas-area\")], [\n    view_reveal_code(model),\n    html.canvas([attr.id(\"animation-canvas\"), attr.class(\"canvas\")]),\n    html.pre([attr.class(\"output\")], [html.text(model.output)]),\n    view_banner(model),\n    view_game_toggle(model),\n    view_controls(model),\n  ])\n}\n\n// The playback controls, in a small box overlaid on the bottom-right corner of\n// the animation pane: a collapse toggle at the top right, then the reset / play /\n// pause buttons with the speed slider, then the shape slider. Collapsing only\n// hides the rows (via CSS), so the sliders stay in the page.\nfn view_controls(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  let #(box_class, toggle_icon, toggle_title) = case model.controls_open {\n    True -> #(\"controls\", \"▾\", \"Hide controls\")\n    False -> #(\"controls collapsed\", \"▸\", \"Show controls\")\n  }\n  html.div([attr.class(box_class)], [\n    html.button(\n      [\n        attr.class(\"controls-toggle\"),\n        attr.title(toggle_title),\n        attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", toggle_title),\n        attr.attribute(\"aria-expanded\", case model.controls_open {\n          True -> \"true\"\n          False -> \"false\"\n        }),\n        event.on_click(ToggleControls),\n      ],\n      [html.text(toggle_icon)],\n    ),\n    html.div([attr.class(\"control\")], [\n      html.div([attr.class(\"playback-buttons\")], [\n        playback_button(\"⟲\", \"Reset\", Reset, \"reset-btn\"),\n        playback_button(\"▶\\u{FE0E}\", \"Play\", Play, \"\"),\n        playback_button(\"❚❚\", \"Pause\", Pause, \"\"),\n      ]),\n      html.input([\n        attr.type_(\"range\"),\n        attr.min(float.to_string(speed_min)),\n        attr.max(float.to_string(speed_max)),\n        attr.step(\"0.1\"),\n        attr.value(float.to_string(model.speed)),\n        attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"speed-slider\"),\n        event.on_input(SetSpeed),\n      ]),\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-value\")], [\n        html.text(float.to_string(float.to_precision(model.speed, 1)) <> \"×\"),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n    html.label([attr.class(\"control\")], [\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-label\")], [html.text(\"shape_slider\")]),\n      html.input([\n        attr.type_(\"range\"),\n        attr.min(int.to_string(shape_slider_min)),\n        attr.max(int.to_string(shape_slider_max)),\n        attr.step(\"1\"),\n        attr.value(int.to_string(model.shape_slider)),\n        attr.attribute(\"data-test-id\", \"shape-slider\"),\n        event.on_input(SetShapeSlider),\n      ]),\n      html.span([attr.class(\"control-value\")], [\n        html.text(int.to_string(model.shape_slider)),\n      ]),\n    ]),\n  ])\n}\n\nfn playback_button(\n  icon: String,\n  label: String,\n  msg: Msg,\n  extra_class: String,\n) -> Element(Msg) {\n  html.button(\n    [\n      attr.class(string.trim(\"playback-btn \" <> extra_class)),\n      attr.title(label),\n      attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", label),\n      event.on_click(msg),\n    ],\n    [html.text(icon)],\n  )\n}\n\n// The button that brings the code column back, overlaid on the top-left of the\n// right pane. Nothing is rendered while the column is already showing (its own\n// header holds the button that hides it).\nfn view_reveal_code(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {\n  case model.code_open {\n    True -> element.none()\n    False ->\n      html.button(\n        [\n          attr.class(\"icon-btn reveal-code\"),\n          attr.attribute(\"title\", \"Show code\"),\n          event.on_click(ToggleCode),\n        ],\n        [html.text(\"⟩\")],\n      )\n  }\n}\n\n// ── Main ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\npub fn main() -> Nil {\n  let app = lustre.application(init, update, view)\n  let assert Ok(_) = lustre.start(app, \"#app\", Nil)\n  Nil\n}\n","new_content_size":31846,"new_hash":"e6a1ddc289545f974bec110dafb890a35a8cf6ff0ccaf2cbba2e185521f17353","old_hash":"a5aefe8426b92e2f0dc3a7dbcfd388b1d39cf2b18aa23131b33f7935e0a1d217","operation":"modify","path":"src/app.gleam"},"hash":"549bfd4e688c3e0ccfde096bc9777cf683ac063b1bd40275cd3bd20816408d66","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"5b4af96e385f2b04771d92c28606889f82b4dbda87379bf49c78dfd86ffa5319","seq":5248,"t":77609989,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:47.523Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":27,"new_content":"//// The game's levels: where the anchor starts, and the red (\"evil\") paths the\n//// player must avoid. See game.gleam for the rules.\n////\n//// Coordinates are the canvas's: (0, 0) is the centre, x and y run from -500 to\n//// 500, and y grows downward, so the top edge is y = -500.\n\nimport gleam/int\nimport shape.{type Path, type Point, Line, Path, Point}\n\npub type Level {\n  Level(\n    name: String,\n    // Where the anchor is when the level starts or restarts.\n    start: Point,\n    // The red paths, timed on the red clock (tenths of a second).\n    red: List(Path),\n    // True: the red paths start over from an empty canvas after they finish.\n    // False: they stay once drawn.\n    loop: Bool,\n    // The code the level starts with, before the player has edited it.\n    starter: String,\n  )\n}\n\n/// The code the numbered levels start with.\npub const starter_code =\n  \"fn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\n  [Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 250)), 0, 10, 4)]\n}\n\nfn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point {\n  Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 200)\n}\n\"\n\n/// The Tutorial level's code: a working climb, explained step by step.\npub const tutorial_code =\n  \"// Tutorial: climb to the green bar at the top without\n// touching a red line or the left and right edges. Press Restart to watch this code win,\n// then try the experiments at the bottom.\n\n// `anchor` is the gold diamond. Every line must start there.\n// y grows downward, so going up means making y smaller.\nfn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\n  // One line from the anchor, 300 units straight up, drawn\n  // between time 0 and time 10 (tenths of a second).\n  [Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300)), 0, 10, 4)]\n}\n\n// Where the anchor goes next: the small dark diamond. Once\n// your line is drawn through it, the anchor jumps there, and\n// draw runs again so your next line starts from it.\nfn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point {\n  Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 250)\n}\n\n// Experiments:\n// 1. In draw, change anchor.x to anchor.x + 300. The line\n//    misses the dark diamond, so the anchor stops moving.\n// 2. Instead, change Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300) to\n//    Point(300, -150). Your line now ends where the red line\n//    is heading, so you are caught when it grows across to it.\n// 3. Change the times 0, 10 to 0, 30. The climb is slower;\n//    watch the red line grow while you wait.\n// 4. Press Pause just after the anchor moves, then Reset.\n//    Your old lines vanish but the anchor stays put.\n\"\n\n/// Every level, in menu order.\n/// PLACEHOLDERS: Level 1 and Level 2 are simple levels that exercise both kinds\n/// of red drawing. The real levels are still to be designed.\npub fn all() -> List(Level) {\n  [\n    // Gentle: one slow red line that grows from the right edge toward the\n    // left, stopping short of the straight climb up the middle.\n    Level(\n      name: \"Tutorial\",\n      start: Point(0.0, 480.0),\n      red: [red(500, -150, 150, -150, 0, 80)],\n      loop: False,\n      starter: tutorial_code,\n    ),\n    Level(\n      name: \"Level 1\",\n      start: Point(0.0, 480.0),\n      red: [\n        red(0, -500, 0, 150, 0, 40),\n        red(0, -500, -350, 100, 5, 45),\n        red(0, -500, 350, 100, 5, 45),\n        red(0, -150, -200, 50, 20, 40),\n      ],\n      loop: False,\n      starter: starter_code,\n    ),\n    Level(\n      name: \"Level 2\",\n      start: Point(0.0, 480.0),\n      red: [\n        red(-300, -500, -300, 200, 0, 20),\n        red(0, -500, 0, 200, 0, 20),\n        red(300, -500, 300, 200, 0, 20),\n      ],\n      loop: True,\n      starter: starter_code,\n    ),\n  ]\n}\n\n// A red line from (x1, y1) to (x2, y2), drawn between start and end.\nfn red(x1: Int, y1: Int, x2: Int, y2: Int, start: Int, end: Int) -> Path {\n  Path(\n    Line(point(x1, y1), point(x2, y2)),\n    int.to_float(start),\n    int.to_float(end),\n    6.0,\n  )\n}\n\nfn point(x: Int, y: Int) -> Point {\n  Point(int.to_float(x), int.to_float(y))\n}\n","new_content_size":3916,"new_hash":"d2176a45aecef8b21a5ecb4a2fb67706edbee3eb1e7671f5e99de80227889afd","old_hash":"0fc0945f67f11af2fe38f09e6d5849e92b76d4e88dd499b35132f8b7e6f373b8","operation":"modify","path":"src/levels.gleam"},"hash":"e26764d148c3e798abe8903b26140611f19dd2dd202f5f24feb0add82e1688fc","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"549bfd4e688c3e0ccfde096bc9777cf683ac063b1bd40275cd3bd20816408d66","seq":5249,"t":77609990,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:47.524Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":89,"new_content":"//// The game's \"How to play\" guide: a short series of steps shown in a panel.\n//// The app keeps track of which step is open; this module holds the steps and\n//// draws the panel, so it never depends back on app.\n\nimport gleam/int\nimport gleam/list\nimport gleam/option.{type Option, None, Some}\nimport gleam/string\nimport lustre/attribute as attr\nimport lustre/element.{type Element}\nimport lustre/element/html\nimport lustre/event\n\n/// One step of the guide. In a paragraph, text between backticks is shown as\n/// code. A snippet is example code, shown read-only beneath the paragraphs.\npub type Step {\n  Step(title: String, paragraphs: List(String), snippet: Option(String))\n}\n\npub fn steps() -> List(Step) {\n  [\n    Step(\n      \"Climb to the top\",\n      [\n        \"Write code whose lines climb from the bottom of the canvas to the green bar at the top.\",\n        \"Red lines grow down from the top. If any of your lines touches a red line, you're caught.\",\n        \"Stay inside the canvas, too: a line that touches the left or right edge loses.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Start at the anchor\",\n      [\n        \"Your lines must start at `anchor`, the gold diamond near the bottom. Lines that start anywhere else are drawn faded and don't count.\",\n        \"y grows downward, so going up means making y smaller.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"fn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\n  [Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300)), 0, 10, 4)]\n}\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Move the anchor up\",\n      [\n        \"One line can't reach the top on its own. Write `move_anchor` to say where the anchor goes next; it's shown as a small dark diamond.\",\n        \"When one of your lines is drawn through that point, the anchor jumps there for good, and draw runs again so your next lines start from it.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"fn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point {\n  Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 250)\n}\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Two clocks\",\n      [\n        \"The red lines run on their own clock, which starts when the level does.\",\n        \"Play, Pause, Reset, and the speed slider control only your lines. The red lines keep growing no matter what you do.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"The reset trick\",\n      [\n        \"When the anchor moves, your old lines stay on screen (dimmer), and red lines can still catch them.\",\n        \"Reset, or a negative speed, clears your old lines, but the anchor stays where it climbed to. So climb, let the anchor move, then reset before the red lines reach what you left behind.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Timing\",\n      [\n        \"A Path's last three numbers are its start time, end time, and thickness. Times are in tenths of a second.\",\n        \"Start a line later, or draw it faster, to slip past a red line before it arrives.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x - 200, anchor.y - 300)), 15, 20, 4)\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Levels\",\n      [\n        \"Pick a level from the menu above the code. In some levels the red lines stay once drawn; in others they clear and grow again in a loop.\",\n        \"Restart puts the anchor back at the start and restarts both clocks. Each level remembers your code.\",\n        \"The Tutorial level is a gentle place to try all of this.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n  ]\n}\n\n/// The guide panel open at step index (counting from 0), over the whole app.\n/// to_tutorial opens the Tutorial level; it is offered on the last step.\npub fn view(\n  index: Int,\n  on_back on_back: msg,\n  on_next on_next: msg,\n  on_close on_close: msg,\n  to_tutorial to_tutorial: msg,\n) -> Element(msg) {\n  let all = steps()\n  let count = list.length(all)\n  let last = index >= count - 1\n  case list.drop(all, index) {\n    [] -> element.none()\n    [step, ..] ->\n      html.div([attr.class(\"guide-backdrop\")], [\n        html.div(\n          [\n            attr.class(\"guide\"),\n            attr.role(\"dialog\"),\n            attr.attribute(\"aria-modal\", \"true\"),\n            attr.attribute(\"aria-labelledby\", \"guide-title\"),\n          ],\n          [\n            html.div([attr.class(\"guide-header\")], [\n              html.span([attr.class(\"guide-kicker\")], [\n                html.text(\"How to play\"),\n              ]),\n              html.button(\n                [\n                  attr.class(\"guide-close\"),\n                  attr.title(\"Close\"),\n                  attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Close\"),\n                  event.on_click(on_close),\n                ],\n                [html.text(\"×\")],\n              ),\n            ]),\n            html.h2([attr.id(\"guide-title\"), attr.class(\"guide-title\")], [\n              html.text(step.title),\n            ]),\n            html.div(\n              [attr.class(\"guide-body\")],\n              list.map(step.paragraphs, fn(p) { html.p([], inline_code(p)) }),\n            ),\n            case step.snippet {\n              Some(code) ->\n                html.pre([attr.class(\"guide-snippet\")], [\n                  html.code([], [html.text(code)]),\n                ])\n              None -> element.none()\n            },\n            html.div([attr.class(\"guide-footer\")], [\n              html.span([attr.class(\"guide-progress\")], [\n                html.text(\n                  \"Step \"\n                  <> int.to_string(index + 1)\n                  <> \" of \"\n                  <> int.to_string(count),\n                ),\n              ]),\n              html.div([attr.class(\"guide-actions\")], [\n                html.button(\n                  [\n                    attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n                    attr.disabled(index == 0),\n                    event.on_click(on_back),\n                  ],\n                  [html.text(\"Back\")],\n                ),\n                case last {\n                  False ->\n                    html.button(\n                      [\n                        attr.class(\"library-btn guide-primary\"),\n                        event.on_click(on_next),\n                      ],\n                      [html.text(\"Next\")],\n                    )\n                  True ->\n                    html.button(\n                      [\n                        attr.class(\"library-btn guide-primary\"),\n                        event.on_click(to_tutorial),\n                      ],\n                      [html.text(\"Try the Tutorial level\")],\n                    )\n                },\n              ]),\n            ]),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ])\n  }\n}\n\n/// The number of steps in the guide.\npub fn count() -> Int {\n  list.length(steps())\n}\n\n// A paragraph's text, with the parts between backticks shown as code.\nfn inline_code(text: String) -> List(Element(msg)) {\n  string.split(text, \"`\")\n  |> list.index_map(fn(part, i) {\n    case i % 2 {\n      0 -> html.text(part)\n      _ -> html.code([attr.class(\"guide-code\")], [html.text(part)])\n    }\n  })\n}\n","new_content_size":6877,"new_hash":"f6f300d4aa9de6907e6e2cac326f97827c33777d166afd088b3becfedc644d6f","old_hash":"ef1017725c381683739657e94fc02f2e242d2cbaec45966cf5e5933873e90215","operation":"modify","path":"src/tutorial.gleam"},"hash":"5f07198e0c50d1464659a32a18c76df643613b47e0f74e5abaea6f235194ed71","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"e26764d148c3e798abe8903b26140611f19dd2dd202f5f24feb0add82e1688fc","seq":5250,"t":77609990,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:47.524Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":171,"new_content":"Extension: \"Climb\" -- a game mode with levels\n\nOverview\nAnimator gains a game mode, turned on with its own toggle and with its own menu of\nthree levels (designed later). In each level, the player writes code whose lines must\nclimb from the bottom of the canvas to the top while dodging \"evil\" drawings: red\npaths, built into the level, that branch out from the top of the canvas toward the\nbottom. Turning the toggle off returns to the normal animator, which works exactly\nas before. Each level's code is saved in localStorage the same way drawings are, so\nit survives a reload.\n\nThe anchor\nEach level starts the player at a point near the bottom of the canvas called the\nanchor. In game mode, code can read a built-in value `anchor`, a Point (like\nshape_slider is an Int). The player's lines must start at the anchor, for example\nPath(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300)), 0, 10, 3). The draw function's\nsignature does not change.\n\nMoving the anchor (in code)\nAlongside draw, the player can write a second function,\nfn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point, that says where the anchor goes next. The\nanchor moves to that point the moment one of the player's visible lines reaches it;\nif no visible line ever reaches it, the anchor stays put. A move is permanent: after\nit, draw and move_anchor are run again with the new anchor, so new lines grow from\nthe new anchor (starting from the beginning of the player's timeline), and climbing\nhigher means chaining moves.\n\nTwo timelines\nThe red drawings and the player's drawing run on separate clocks. The red clock runs\non its own from the moment the level starts. The playback controls (Reset, Play,\nPause, and the speed slider, including negative speeds) control only the player's\nclock. When the anchor moves, the player's old lines stay on screen -- and can still\nbe caught -- until the player presses Reset or sets a negative speed, which clears\nthem. So a strategy is to grow a line upward, let the anchor move to its tip, then\nquickly reset so the red drawings have nothing to hit, while keeping the higher\nanchor. A separate \"Restart level\" button resets both clocks and puts the anchor back\nat the level's starting point.\n\nRed drawings\nLevels can differ in how their red paths behave over time: in some, red paths grow\ndown from the top at normal speed and stay on screen once drawn, so the level gets\nharder the longer the player takes; in others, the red paths grow down, clear, and\nstart again from the top in a loop.\n\nWinning and losing\nThe player wins when one of their visible lines reaches the green goal bar that fills\nthe top of the canvas. The player is caught when any visible part of one of their\nlines (old or new) touches any visible part of a red path at the same moment, and\nalso loses if one of their lines touches the left or right edge of the canvas; line\nthickness counts. Whichever happens first ends the attempt: the clocks stop and a\nbanner says \"Level complete!\", \"Caught!\", or \"Hit the edge!\". Restart level tries\nagain.\n\nDisplay\nPlayer lines use the normal color, red paths are red, the goal is a solid green bar\nalong the top, and the anchor is shown on the canvas as a small marker so the player\ncan see where their next lines start.\n\nTutorial\nThe first time game mode is turned on, a \"How to play\" guide opens: seven short steps\n(the goal, the anchor, move_anchor, the two clocks, the reset trick, timing, and\nlevels), some with read-only example code. Both clocks pause while it is open, and a\n\"How to play\" button reopens it. Its last step leads to a Tutorial level, first in the\nlevel menu, with one slow red line out of the way and commented starter code that\nwins as written and suggests experiments to try.\n\nImplementation notes\nThe levels (starting anchor, red paths, and whether they loop) are Gleam data in a new\nmodule. The app binds `anchor` as an extra built-in in the global frame and calls\nmove_anchor with the existing evaluator.run, just as drawing.gleam calls draw, so\nthe interpreter itself is unchanged. The canvas code is extended to keep two clocks\nand draw two layers, and the app checks for anchor moves, wins, and hits as the\nclocks advance.\n","new_content_size":4157,"new_hash":"4d2524fdfbb0e912f113d3c6f62282d438f61cd98e07ed8febcfabe75e5dadbf","old_hash":"1d75c8f88929dffdd08ed05f7e0965796dcc151a9a7eb4eb3d73ddc6cba1b610","operation":"modify","path":"extension.txt"},"hash":"68f97d429486637e0b13b7f7da771609229ce2decf6e271ba0ec1ee013828346","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"5f07198e0c50d1464659a32a18c76df643613b47e0f74e5abaea6f235194ed71","seq":5251,"t":77610186,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:29:47.720Z"}
