{"data":{"diff_size":204,"new_content":"Extension: \"Climb\" -- a game mode with levels\n\nOverview\nAnimator gains a game mode, turned on with its own toggle and with its own menu of\nthree levels (designed later). In each level, the player writes code whose lines must\nclimb from the bottom of the canvas to the top while dodging \"evil\" drawings: red\npaths, built into the level, that branch out from the top of the canvas toward the\nbottom. Turning the toggle off returns to the normal animator, which works exactly\nas before. Each level's code is saved in localStorage the same way drawings are, so\nit survives a reload.\n\nThe anchor\nEach level starts the player at a point near the bottom of the canvas called the\nanchor. In game mode, code can read a built-in value `anchor`, a Point (like\nshape_slider is an Int). The player's lines must start at the anchor, for example\nPath(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300)), 0, 10, 3). The draw function's\nsignature does not change.\n\nMoving the anchor (in code)\nAlongside draw, the player can write a second function,\nfn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point, that says where the anchor goes next. The\nanchor moves to that point the moment one of the player's visible lines reaches it;\nif no visible line ever reaches it, the anchor stays put. A built-in,\nfind_anchor(start, end, thickness), returns a Path drawn straight from the anchor to\nthe point move_anchor names, so a player can write [find_anchor(0, 10, 4)] to climb\nfrom anchor to anchor. A move is permanent: after\nit, draw and move_anchor are run again with the new anchor, so new lines grow from\nthe new anchor (starting from the beginning of the player's timeline), and climbing\nhigher means chaining moves.\n\nTwo timelines\nThe red drawings and the player's drawing run on separate clocks. The red clock runs\non its own from the moment the level starts. The playback controls (Reset, Play,\nPause, and the speed slider, including negative speeds) control only the player's\nclock. When the anchor moves, the player's old lines stay on screen -- and can still\nbe caught -- until the player presses Reset or sets a negative speed, which clears\nthem. So a strategy is to grow a line upward, let the anchor move to its tip, then\nquickly reset so the red drawings have nothing to hit, while keeping the higher\nanchor. A separate \"Restart level\" button resets both clocks and puts the anchor back\nat the level's starting point.\n\nRed drawings\nLevels can differ in how their red paths behave over time: in some, red paths grow\ndown from the top at normal speed and stay on screen once drawn, so the level gets\nharder the longer the player takes; in others, the red paths grow down, clear, and\nstart again from the top in a loop.\n\nWinning and losing\nThe player wins when one of their visible lines reaches the green goal bar that fills\nthe top of the canvas. The player is caught when any visible part of one of their\nlines (old or new) touches any visible part of a red path at the same moment, and\nalso loses if one of their lines touches the left or right edge of the canvas; line\nthickness counts. Whichever happens first ends the attempt: the clocks stop and a\nbanner says \"Level complete!\", \"Caught!\", or \"Hit the edge!\". Restart level tries\nagain.\n\nDisplay\nPlayer lines use the normal color, red paths are red, the goal is a solid green bar\nalong the top, and the anchor is shown on the canvas as a small marker so the player\ncan see where their next lines start.\n\nTutorial\nThe first time game mode is turned on, a \"How to play\" guide opens: seven short steps\n(the goal, the anchor, move_anchor, the two clocks, the reset trick, timing, and\nlevels), some with read-only example code. Both clocks pause while it is open, and a\n\"How to play\" button reopens it. Its last step leads to a Tutorial level, first in the\nlevel menu, with one slow red line out of the way and commented starter code that\nwins as written and suggests experiments to try.\n\nImplementation notes\nThe levels (starting anchor, red paths, and whether they loop) are Gleam data in a new\nmodule. The app binds `anchor` as an extra built-in in the global frame and calls\nmove_anchor with the existing evaluator.run, just as drawing.gleam calls draw, so\nthe interpreter itself is unchanged. The canvas code is extended to keep two clocks\nand draw two layers, and the app checks for anchor moves, wins, and hits as the\nclocks advance.\n","new_content_size":4361,"new_hash":"4c8b0bbd28e64765ba91f97ddcdd977b065be13e87edbfb44df4600882ba90f1","old_hash":"4d2524fdfbb0e912f113d3c6f62282d438f61cd98e07ed8febcfabe75e5dadbf","operation":"modify","path":"extension.txt"},"hash":"875c67d286eeb7b0a52f5b731dee02d9fef96daa3f3bbfdd6836cc7ff3589bb1","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"af2c5991f4183c2033faf180210a1a24730a6d5659d7a401145cc96ffa7b6f8a","seq":5297,"t":78349122,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:42:06.634Z"}
{"data":{"diff_size":172,"new_content":"//// The game's \"How to play\" guide: a short series of steps shown in a panel.\n//// The app keeps track of which step is open; this module holds the steps and\n//// draws the panel, so it never depends back on app.\n\nimport gleam/int\nimport gleam/list\nimport gleam/option.{type Option, None, Some}\nimport gleam/string\nimport lustre/attribute as attr\nimport lustre/element.{type Element}\nimport lustre/element/html\nimport lustre/event\n\n/// One step of the guide. In a paragraph, text between backticks is shown as\n/// code. A snippet is example code, shown read-only beneath the paragraphs.\npub type Step {\n  Step(title: String, paragraphs: List(String), snippet: Option(String))\n}\n\npub fn steps() -> List(Step) {\n  [\n    Step(\n      \"Climb to the top\",\n      [\n        \"Write code whose lines climb from the bottom of the canvas to the green bar at the top.\",\n        \"Red lines grow down from the top. If any of your lines touches a red line, you're caught.\",\n        \"Stay inside the canvas, too: a line that touches the left or right edge loses.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Start at the anchor\",\n      [\n        \"Your lines must start at `anchor`, the gold diamond near the bottom. Lines that start anywhere else are drawn faded and don't count.\",\n        \"y grows downward, so going up means making y smaller.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"fn draw(shape_slider: Int) -> List(Path) {\n  [Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 300)), 0, 10, 4)]\n}\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Move the anchor up\",\n      [\n        \"One line can't reach the top on its own. Write `move_anchor` to say where the anchor goes next; it's shown as a small dark diamond.\",\n        \"When one of your lines is drawn through that point, the anchor jumps there for good, and draw runs again so your next lines start from it.\",\n        \"Shortcut: `find_anchor(start, end, thickness)` is a straight line from the anchor to the next anchor, so `[find_anchor(0, 10, 4)]` climbs from anchor to anchor.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"fn move_anchor(anchor: Point) -> Point {\n  Point(anchor.x, anchor.y - 250)\n}\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Two clocks\",\n      [\n        \"The red lines run on their own clock, which starts when the level does.\",\n        \"Play, Pause, Reset, and the speed slider control only your lines. The red lines keep growing no matter what you do.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"The reset trick\",\n      [\n        \"When the anchor moves, your old lines stay on screen (dimmer), and red lines can still catch them.\",\n        \"Reset, or a negative speed, clears your old lines, but the anchor stays where it climbed to. So climb, let the anchor move, then reset before the red lines reach what you left behind.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Timing\",\n      [\n        \"A Path's last three numbers are its start time, end time, and thickness. Times are in tenths of a second.\",\n        \"Start a line later, or draw it faster, to slip past a red line before it arrives.\",\n      ],\n      Some(\n        \"Path(Line(anchor, Point(anchor.x - 200, anchor.y - 300)), 15, 20, 4)\",\n      ),\n    ),\n    Step(\n      \"Levels\",\n      [\n        \"Pick a level from the menu above the code. In some levels the red lines stay once drawn; in others they clear and grow again in a loop.\",\n        \"Restart puts the anchor back at the start and restarts both clocks. Each level remembers your code.\",\n        \"The Tutorial level is a gentle place to try all of this.\",\n      ],\n      None,\n    ),\n  ]\n}\n\n/// The guide panel open at step index (counting from 0), over the whole app.\n/// to_tutorial opens the Tutorial level; it is offered on the last step.\npub fn view(\n  index: Int,\n  on_back on_back: msg,\n  on_next on_next: msg,\n  on_close on_close: msg,\n  to_tutorial to_tutorial: msg,\n) -> Element(msg) {\n  let all = steps()\n  let count = list.length(all)\n  let last = index >= count - 1\n  case list.drop(all, index) {\n    [] -> element.none()\n    [step, ..] ->\n      html.div([attr.class(\"guide-backdrop\")], [\n        html.div(\n          [\n            attr.class(\"guide\"),\n            attr.role(\"dialog\"),\n            attr.attribute(\"aria-modal\", \"true\"),\n            attr.attribute(\"aria-labelledby\", \"guide-title\"),\n          ],\n          [\n            html.div([attr.class(\"guide-header\")], [\n              html.span([attr.class(\"guide-kicker\")], [\n                html.text(\"How to play\"),\n              ]),\n              html.button(\n                [\n                  attr.class(\"guide-close\"),\n                  attr.title(\"Close\"),\n                  attr.attribute(\"aria-label\", \"Close\"),\n                  event.on_click(on_close),\n                ],\n                [html.text(\"×\")],\n              ),\n            ]),\n            html.h2([attr.id(\"guide-title\"), attr.class(\"guide-title\")], [\n              html.text(step.title),\n            ]),\n            html.div(\n              [attr.class(\"guide-body\")],\n              list.map(step.paragraphs, fn(p) { html.p([], inline_code(p)) }),\n            ),\n            case step.snippet {\n              Some(code) ->\n                html.pre([attr.class(\"guide-snippet\")], [\n                  html.code([], [html.text(code)]),\n                ])\n              None -> element.none()\n            },\n            html.div([attr.class(\"guide-footer\")], [\n              html.span([attr.class(\"guide-progress\")], [\n                html.text(\n                  \"Step \"\n                  <> int.to_string(index + 1)\n                  <> \" of \"\n                  <> int.to_string(count),\n                ),\n              ]),\n              html.div([attr.class(\"guide-actions\")], [\n                html.button(\n                  [\n                    attr.class(\"library-btn\"),\n                    attr.disabled(index == 0),\n                    event.on_click(on_back),\n                  ],\n                  [html.text(\"Back\")],\n                ),\n                case last {\n                  False ->\n                    html.button(\n                      [\n                        attr.class(\"library-btn guide-primary\"),\n                        event.on_click(on_next),\n                      ],\n                      [html.text(\"Next\")],\n                    )\n                  True ->\n                    html.button(\n                      [\n                        attr.class(\"library-btn guide-primary\"),\n                        event.on_click(to_tutorial),\n                      ],\n                      [html.text(\"Try the Tutorial level\")],\n                    )\n                },\n              ]),\n            ]),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ])\n  }\n}\n\n/// The number of steps in the guide.\npub fn count() -> Int {\n  list.length(steps())\n}\n\n// A paragraph's text, with the parts between backticks shown as code.\nfn inline_code(text: String) -> List(Element(msg)) {\n  string.split(text, \"`\")\n  |> list.index_map(fn(part, i) {\n    case i % 2 {\n      0 -> html.text(part)\n      _ -> html.code([attr.class(\"guide-code\")], [html.text(part)])\n    }\n  })\n}\n","new_content_size":7049,"new_hash":"a58c4021cd16369269f60964988388b5d09c8338f0e7c1d3688520e4721a1f0b","old_hash":"f6f300d4aa9de6907e6e2cac326f97827c33777d166afd088b3becfedc644d6f","operation":"modify","path":"src/tutorial.gleam"},"hash":"0910d76899907aae61cfd307dde332804e192d8d911308cc56c3fb9b3724bc5d","kind":"fs.external_change","prev_hash":"875c67d286eeb7b0a52f5b731dee02d9fef96daa3f3bbfdd6836cc7ff3589bb1","seq":5298,"t":78349123,"wall":"2026-11-15T23:42:06.634Z"}
